WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.536
In this lesson, you'll tackle a real scaling challenge.

2
00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:07.941
What happens when your agent has access to hundreds of tools?

3
00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:11.444
Instead of stuffing every tool definition into the prompt,

4
00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.114
You'll treat tools as procedural memory,

5
00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.850
storing them in a memory backed store

6
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.152
and retrieving only the relevant ones

7
00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:21.554
at inference time using semantic search.

8
00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.857
All right, let's go.

9
00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.960
What we're going to cover on this lesson is

10
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.765
how large language models are able to become tool-aware for task completion,

11
00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:34.267
how they are able to perceive the tools

12
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.569
that they have at their disposal.

13
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.873
We'll also talk about the Toolbox pattern,

14
00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:43.576
which is a pattern that allows large language models to become aware

15
00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.879
of all the tools that they have

16
00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:49.582
and how the tools are treated as retrievable sources

17
00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.185
rather than putting all of them into the context.

18
00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:55.588
And how we can use embeddings and semantic search

19
00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.591
to allow these large language models to select

20
00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:01.594
their most optimal tools at any time.

21
00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:05.031
Tool calling is a technique or a pattern

22
00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:08.835
where the large language model doesn't directly execute the code.

23
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.439
But rather, it will output a structured request

24
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:16.609
so that the environment will actually execute the code

25
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.013
and then return it so the large language model has

26
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:23.516
this information available to return the information to the user.

27
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.453
In a typical scenario, what you will have

28
00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:29.155
is a lot of tool definitions with a name,

29
00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.758
a description, and some parameters

30
00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.461
on how this tool has to be called.

31
00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:38.431
And then we'll also have a user query, like for instance,

32
00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.101
What is the weather like in London tomorrow?

33
00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:43.970
The large language model will be able to

34
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.473
output this structured request which will execute

35
00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.374
within the environment.

36
00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.277
Then the system will execute the tool,

37
00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:54.280
return the response to the large language model,

38
00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:57.283
and then through this response we will be able to

39
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.586
generate the correct response to the user.

40
00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:02.689
But this has some limitations.

41
00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.391
We typically have a lot of tools

42
00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.694
that we want to use on our environment.

43
00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:11.197
And this is of course very beneficial for the large language model

44
00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.900
because the more information and the more tools

45
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.369
that a large language model has at their disposal,

46
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:19.072
the better the responses will be, right?

47
00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:22.408
But whenever you have too many tools,

48
00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:25.612
this has some obvious disadvantages, right?

49
00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:30.383
The context size is limited on large language models,

50
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.586
so we obviously cannot stuff all of the tools,

51
00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:38.091
their names, their parameters, and their descriptions into the context

52
00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.593
without having some problems.

53
00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:44.097
So putting all of them into the context window

54
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.932
can cause a lot of agents

55
00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.101
to fail in a number of ways.

56
00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:52.805
These are some of the most obvious disadvantages of this.

57
00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:56.276
First of all, we have Context Confusion and Context Bloat,

58
00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:58.978
which is that whenever a large language model

59
00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.281
sees a lot of these tools at the same time,

60
00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.184
the context will get overwhelmed

61
00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.486
by the amount of information from the tools

62
00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:09.489
and will have less context available

63
00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:12.792
for the input and the actual output being produced.

64
00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:16.529
We also have this thing called Tool Selection Degradation,

65
00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.631
which means that the responses from the

66
00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.833
large language model will degrade as a consequence.

67
00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:25.838
This will also cause the latency and the tokens to increase.

68
00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:30.410
So you will suffer the consequences of having to pay more tokens.

69
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.146
and also the latency can increase

70
00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:35.848
because the large language model will actually

71
00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:39.052
take longer to produce a response for all

72
00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:42.055
the amount of information it needs to process beforehand,

73
00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:45.358
which in general will cause a performance degradation.

74
00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:48.461
So, a way for us to scale this system

75
00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:53.461
so that it's more beneficial for the large language model is this.

76
00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:56.736
First, we can have all the names, descriptions,

77
00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.138
and parameters for each one of the tools.

78
00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:01.674
So if we encode that information

79
00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:04.277
and put that into a vector space

80
00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.580
by creating an encoding of this information

81
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.483
and passing it through an Embedding Model,

82
00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.086
we can create an embedding of the Name,

83
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:15.688
the Description, and the Parameters of the tools.

84
00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.057
And then we can use this Embedding as

85
00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:20.260
well as the original information from the tool

86
00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.662
and load this into the database.

87
00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:28.129
So we can perform semantic search operations and similarity search on it.

88
00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:32.639
so that the large language model, after it receives a user query,

89
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:36.643
for instance, our original query, what is the weather like in London,

90
00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.346
we can take that input and we will perform similarity search

91
00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:43.149
against the embedding that we just created.

92
00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.918
So after we have our embeddings,

93
00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.121
we can take the user query

94
00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:51.424
and perform similarity search against this embedding.

95
00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.827
So semantic search can be executed

96
00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:57.530
and the best tools for resolving

97
00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.832
this user query will be selected.

98
00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:03.102
Then after the right tool is selected,

99
00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.805
the large language model will be able to call this tool,

100
00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:09.709
execute it, and return a result to the large language model.

101
00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.345
So, as you see on the bottom left,

102
00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:15.548
this is where a representation of our embedding space.

103
00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:18.151
We will have all these tools on this space.

104
00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.853
And then, by performing similarity search,

105
00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:24.557
we will find the closest tools to the user query.

106
00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:28.561
Now, this is a great idea, but it might not be enough.

107
00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:31.631
So we propose another type of enhancement,

108
00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:34.867
which we call the Memory Unit Augmentation,

109
00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:37.937
which is a little bit of a different strategy

110
00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:42.542
where we will take the tool definitions, the name, and the description,

111
00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.945
and run them through a large language model

112
00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:48.748
and enhance this name and description.

113
00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:52.452
And then we will use this enhanced name and description,

114
00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:55.455
encode it, pass it through an embedding model,

115
00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:58.358
and then create an embedding out of that.

116
00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:02.962
And after this, we will have an augmented tool definition.

117
00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:05.698
which will be loaded into the database.

118
00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:08.601
So we will basically let the large language model

119
00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:11.404
enhance our tool definitions.

120
00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.607
And this has some obvious advantages.

121
00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:17.844
For instance, this will make it so that

122
00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:21.848
in the embedding space, when we perform semantic search now,

123
00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:26.152
there will be higher and better separability between the tools.

124
00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.721
Also, there will be higher recall

125
00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:30.823
and a high-signal embedding text,

126
00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.425
which means that all the things

127
00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:34.827
that we have put into this embedding

128
00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.330
will have better characteristics

129
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.233
and they will be more discernible by the model.

130
00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:43.136
We are going to dive deep into

131
00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:45.438
agent tool use with semantic tool memory.

132
00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:50.643
So, as we did before, we need to quickly connect again

133
00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:53.346
into our Oracle database

134
00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:55.948
to get access into the system.

135
00:06:56.282 --> 00:07:00.787
And as we'll see, we are already connected again into our database

136
00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.790
and the connection seems to be working fine.

137
00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.793
Then we're going to reload our embedding model,

138
00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:09.262
which is the same that we were using before,

139
00:07:09.462 --> 00:07:11.764
the paraphrase-mpnet-base-v2

140
00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:13.866
to create our embeddings.

141
00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.504
And now that we're going to start using our large language models,

142
00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:22.475
we're going to instantiate our OpenAI client

143
00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:25.878
so we can interact with the large language model programmatically.

144
00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.448
Then, as we did before,

145
00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:32.051
we're going to set up our conversational knowledge,

146
00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.854
workflow, and all the tables that we

147
00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:37.457
were using before for the store manager.

148
00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:40.359
And we're also going to provision

149
00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:44.664
our tool log history and our conversation history tables

150
00:07:44.664 --> 00:07:47.366
to be able to access them as well.

151
00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.368
And since we were already running it,

152
00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:51.070
you will see that they already exist,

153
00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.873
but if you hadn't, you will get a notification

154
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:56.676
that they were properly created on this step.

155
00:07:57.076 --> 00:08:00.213
And here we will also be

156
00:08:00.213 --> 00:08:03.883
instantiating our StoreManager as we did before.

157
00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:08.120
And after the creation of the StoreManager instance,

158
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.957
as we did before, we will get all the objects

159
00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:13.926
for the individual stores via the manager

160
00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:15.928
functions that we created.

161
00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:18.931
And after we run this, you'll see

162
00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:23.336
that all the stores are properly loaded via the StoreManager.

163
00:08:23.769 --> 00:08:28.241
And now we begin with the actual code for our chapter.

164
00:08:28.741 --> 00:08:32.678
What we want to do first is instantiate the MemoryManager

165
00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:35.114
as we did before, and then we're

166
00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:37.583
going to initialize our Toolbox pattern object.

167
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.354
The purpose of the Toolbox is to actually load

168
00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:44.257
and store all the different tools that

169
00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:46.859
the LLM is going to have access to.

170
00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.462
So we need to pass the memory_manager,

171
00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:52.465
the OpenAI client instance that we created,

172
00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:54.901
so that it has access to

173
00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:57.370
do some augmentations which we will see,

174
00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.773
and the embedding_model so that the Toolbox

175
00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:04.777
is also able to perform vector similarity search on the toolbox object.

176
00:09:05.111 --> 00:09:07.213
So now that we have executed

177
00:09:07.213 --> 00:09:10.182
and correctly initialized our toolbox object,

178
00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:12.685
we can now see that the memory

179
00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:15.087
manager and the toolbox have been correctly initialized.

180
00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:17.356
So what you are going to do

181
00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:18.958
now is to register a new tool

182
00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:21.661
into the toolbox pattern.

183
00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:24.564
so that the large language model is able to

184
00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:26.766
confidently select it when it needs to.

185
00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:30.169
So what we are doing here is to register a new tool

186
00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:33.072
with a type of parameter

187
00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.775
that lets us augment this via the

188
00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:39.178
LLM enhancement that we saw on this chapter.

189
00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:42.982
So we prepared this function called read_toolbox.

190
00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:47.019
which takes an input query as the user's input,

191
00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:50.690
and also a number which can be any number.

192
00:09:50.923 --> 00:09:53.993
And this value represents however many tools you want

193
00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:59.093
the similarity search to return when querying the toolbox.

194
00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:01.867
So after you run this,

195
00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:04.170
you will be able to see that

196
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.572
the registration of a tool has been

197
00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:08.674
properly executed.

198
00:10:09.175 --> 00:10:11.577
And this performs similarity search

199
00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:14.580
on our toolbox table in the database.

200
00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:18.117
So after we have defined the function,

201
00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:23.517
we will be able to use the toolbox registration tool function

202
00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:28.127
to register more tools and also read from the toolbox.

203
00:10:29.128 --> 00:10:31.530
So after we have created the toolbox pattern

204
00:10:31.831 --> 00:10:34.166
and have a function ready to register

205
00:10:34.166 --> 00:10:36.502
tools and also read from the toolbox,

206
00:10:36.802 --> 00:10:39.805
what we are going to do is create a new tool,

207
00:10:40.106 --> 00:10:41.807
which is through Tavily.

208
00:10:43.542 --> 00:10:46.545
Tavily is a service that allows us to search on the web

209
00:10:46.545 --> 00:10:50.850
and store these results into the knowledge base on our toolbox.

210
00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.819
So for that, we first need to instantiate

211
00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.822
the TavilyClient through their library,

212
00:10:56.822 --> 00:11:00.526
and then we will use our register_tool hint

213
00:11:00.526 --> 00:11:02.528
to create this new tool

214
00:11:02.528 --> 00:11:06.132
so that every time we want to use Tavily,

215
00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:09.468
we will be redirected to this function

216
00:11:09.468 --> 00:11:12.471
called search_tavily which, as before,

217
00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:16.442
will return a maximum number of results by default five.

218
00:11:16.876 --> 00:11:20.279
and we'll perform this query against the Tavily client

219
00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:25.084
through their API. In here, what we are doing

220
00:11:25.084 --> 00:11:26.886
is searching with the Tavily client

221
00:11:26.886 --> 00:11:28.888
on the query that we want.

222
00:11:29.722 --> 00:11:33.025
and limiting the results to our max_results.

223
00:11:33.626 --> 00:11:35.628
After we get our results object,

224
00:11:35.728 --> 00:11:39.031
we will write this result into our knowledge base.

225
00:11:39.432 --> 00:11:43.636
For that, we'll create some text content to embed

226
00:11:43.936 --> 00:11:47.940
in which we will get the title, the content, and the URL

227
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.744
and create some additional metadata associated to it.

228
00:11:52.011 --> 00:11:54.113
For instance, the title,

229
00:11:54.113 --> 00:11:56.816
the url from which we extracted the information,

230
00:11:56.816 --> 00:12:01.816
a score representing the verifiability of the information,

231
00:12:01.821 --> 00:12:04.523
the query, and the timestamp from our query.

232
00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:08.394
and we put all this information into the knowledge base.

233
00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:11.397
And in this cell, what you are going to do

234
00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:13.766
is see the live difference between

235
00:12:13.866 --> 00:12:16.368
actually using the original docstring,

236
00:12:16.669 --> 00:12:20.673
which is what the actual creator of the function used

237
00:12:20.873 --> 00:12:23.676
versus actually allowing the tool definition

238
00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:27.880
and the name to be enhanced by the large language model.

239
00:12:28.414 --> 00:12:31.917
So, for that, we're going to take the tool by name,

240
00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:35.421
which is a tool that we just registered before.

241
00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:38.424
We're going to take its source and we're going to compare

242
00:12:38.424 --> 00:12:42.228
the original docstring with the augmented docstring

243
00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.630
generated by the OpenAI model.

244
00:12:44.997 --> 00:12:47.500
And as you will see here,

245
00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.503
rather than having the original docstring,

246
00:12:50.503 --> 00:12:54.507
you will get an augmented and LLM-enhanced docstring,

247
00:12:54.507 --> 00:12:56.509
which not only takes the description,

248
00:12:56.509 --> 00:12:59.512
it runs and creates a step-by-step call

249
00:12:59.812 --> 00:13:02.815
a definition of each one of the parameters

250
00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:06.018
and the return values for the function.

251
00:13:06.619 --> 00:13:09.722
So, this will allow the large language model

252
00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:12.925
to store this definition into the database.

253
00:13:13.259 --> 00:13:16.262
and the results will be much more separable,

254
00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:19.465
which will allow our similarity search functionality

255
00:13:19.498 --> 00:13:23.169
to make these functions much more distinguishable

256
00:13:23.169 --> 00:13:25.271
for the large language model to use.

257
00:13:25.671 --> 00:13:27.406
So what you are going to

258
00:13:27.406 --> 00:13:29.475
do here is create a local tool,

259
00:13:29.675 --> 00:13:32.678
which will use local Python code,

260
00:13:32.678 --> 00:13:36.148
like using the datetime library locally,

261
00:13:36.182 --> 00:13:41.182
rather than going through an external API third-party service like Tavily.

262
00:13:41.854 --> 00:13:44.657
Each tool can be implemented either externally

263
00:13:45.157 --> 00:13:48.961
or locally. If you run it locally like this,

264
00:13:48.961 --> 00:13:51.363
you can create as many tools as you want.

265
00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:53.766
And if you use a third-party service,

266
00:13:53.766 --> 00:13:57.369
you'll have to integrate with whichever services are available.

267
00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.939
And on this cell, we're going

268
00:13:59.939 --> 00:14:02.374
to take a look at the ArxivRetriever,

269
00:14:02.374 --> 00:14:04.677
which is an automatic integration

270
00:14:04.677 --> 00:14:06.679
by the langchain_community package,

271
00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:10.049
which allows us to retrieve arXiv papers

272
00:14:10.049 --> 00:14:12.852
with a maximum number of papers.

273
00:14:13.252 --> 00:14:17.056
as well as a maximum number of characters per document.

274
00:14:17.356 --> 00:14:20.092
So in this case, what we will do is use

275
00:14:20.092 --> 00:14:22.094
the ArxivRetriever object

276
00:14:22.094 --> 00:14:25.030
from the langchain_community to get some

277
00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:29.435
of these papers. So we will set up an ArxivRetriever object

278
00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:34.139
which will allow us to retrieve arXiv papers in the future.

279
00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:39.144
In this cell, we're going to register an arXiv discovery tool.

280
00:14:39.445 --> 00:14:41.747
So, for instance, we are going to

281
00:14:41.847 --> 00:14:43.849
create a function first of all

282
00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.752
to process the arXiv URLs

283
00:14:46.752 --> 00:14:49.255
and actually get the paper URI,

284
00:14:49.255 --> 00:14:51.257
the identifier of the paper.

285
00:14:51.757 --> 00:14:54.460
And then, in the search candidates function,

286
00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:58.063
we're going to search through arXiv and return

287
00:14:58.063 --> 00:15:00.466
a list of JSON candidates

288
00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:03.269
with their IDs and metadata

289
00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:06.272
so that we can then use this information

290
00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:09.275
to augment the large language model

291
00:15:09.275 --> 00:15:12.778
with external third-party information from arXiv.

292
00:15:13.279 --> 00:15:16.482
this case, we are getting a list of documents

293
00:15:16.482 --> 00:15:19.485
and for each one of the documents, we are appending

294
00:15:19.485 --> 00:15:21.587
and getting the metadata,

295
00:15:21.587 --> 00:15:25.090
the entry ID, which is the identifier of the paper,

296
00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:27.092
and a list of possible candidates

297
00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:30.062
which are the closest to the actual user's query.

298
00:15:30.696 --> 00:15:34.199
And now we're going to see how to perform deep ingestion,

299
00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.902
which not only allows us to use

300
00:15:36.902 --> 00:15:39.305
the previous ArxivLoader from the previous cell,

301
00:15:39.505 --> 00:15:42.207
but also normalize all the information

302
00:15:42.508 --> 00:15:45.210
and actually insert this information

303
00:15:45.511 --> 00:15:48.213
through loading the full paper information,

304
00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.583
the metadata that we extracted from the previous cell,

305
00:15:51.583 --> 00:15:56.388
chunking this information through the RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter,

306
00:15:56.388 --> 00:15:59.191
which is just a way to perform chunking

307
00:15:59.191 --> 00:16:01.894
with a little bit of overlap to improve

308
00:16:01.894 --> 00:16:04.396
each one of the chunks quality

309
00:16:04.396 --> 00:16:07.399
and reduce confusion on the context.

310
00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:12.633
And after storing these chunks into our vector store from Oracle,

311
00:16:13.439 --> 00:16:15.841
we will be able to write this information

312
00:16:16.141 --> 00:16:19.645
into our memory manager to increment the information

313
00:16:20.145 --> 00:16:23.449
and the vectors made available by the toolbox.

314
00:16:23.782 --> 00:16:25.884
And now for the moment of truth.

315
00:16:25.884 --> 00:16:29.288
We are going to test the toolbox functionalities

316
00:16:29.288 --> 00:16:31.590
that we integrated in the chapter.

317
00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.593
So we are going to ask the memory_manager

318
00:16:34.593 --> 00:16:36.595
to read the toolbox object

319
00:16:36.595 --> 00:16:38.597
that we have in our database

320
00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:40.766
and we are going to ask it

321
00:16:40.766 --> 00:16:43.302
to get us more details on AI papers.

322
00:16:43.302 --> 00:16:46.605
And for this, we are going to limit our responses

323
00:16:46.605 --> 00:16:48.574
to a maximum of one.

324
00:16:48.974 --> 00:16:52.344
So the best available toolbox to retrieve the information

325
00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:55.647
for this user query will be printed.

326
00:16:56.648 --> 00:16:58.250
And as we can see here,

327
00:16:58.751 --> 00:17:01.820
the best tool by performing similarity search

328
00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.857
against the toolbox table on our database

329
00:17:04.857 --> 00:17:09.461
is the fetch_and_save_paper_to_kb_db, which is the name that we associated

330
00:17:09.461 --> 00:17:13.165
to get information from the arXiv.

331
00:17:13.966 --> 00:17:17.469
Feel free to customize this and test with different values of K,

332
00:17:17.469 --> 00:17:21.440
so you will see the best tools available for different queries.
